From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Project Luma <builds@project-luma.local>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 22:10:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] luma: dock folders, a Stack and a Grid

A folder can be pinned to the Dock. Its tile sits after the applications,
behind the same divider token the Dash already draws between the parts of a
bar, so the row reads as three things instead of one long strip: what you
run, what you keep, what is happening.

The tile is the folder's own icon, the one Filer draws: a special folder
keeps its own, and so does a folder the person gave an icon to. It comes
from the attributes Filer reads, so the two cannot disagree. A count appears
only when something arrived that has not been looked at, and it clears when
the folder is opened; it is the Dash's own badge, fill, ring and geometry.

Clicking a tile opens one surface with two arrangements, both Filer's: a
Stack, which is Filer's list, and a Grid, which is Filer's icon view. Their
metrics are not chosen here, they are copied: the row height, the cell
radius, the padding, the spacing, the icon size, the type and the number of
name lines all come from Filer's own style.css and nautilus-grid-cell.blp,
quoted beside each constant so the two cannot drift apart. The items carry
Filer's thumbnails too, read from the same cache Filer fills -- never
generated here, because a decoder on the compositor's main loop is not worth
a popover -- resolved only for what is on screen plus a margin, and replaced
in place so nothing jumps when a picture arrives.

Nothing wears a focus ring at rest. Focus opens on the surface, and the
first arrow key moves it onto an item: a ring on an item that nobody chose
reads as a selection, and nothing is selected until the person selects it.

A click anywhere outside an attached surface closes it, and the click still
reaches what it was aimed at. A press captured at the stage often arrives
with no source -- the wallpaper and the Dash both do -- so the actor under
the pointer is asked for, and a press that cannot be placed is a press
somewhere else. Treating an unplaceable press as "not outside" had stopped
click-away working at all. The arrangement is a segmented control in the header
and is remembered for that folder. The surface hangs from its own tile on
whichever edge the Dash is on, and can break out of the dock -- from the pin
or by dragging the header past 26px -- to park anywhere until it is closed.

The divider in front of the rail is the Dash's own token, DIVIDER_WIDTH and
SHELF_DIVIDER_LENGTH from shelfMetrics.js, so it is the same hairline as the
one between the parts of any other bar and follows it when it changes.

Married or separated is the arrangement model's own vocabulary (ADR-044):
married the rail is a part of the dock island; separated it is the 'folders'
island, placed and moved like any other, and the divider goes because the gap
is then doing the separating. It is one rail either way -- the actor moves,
nothing is copied.

Every count, size and time is read from the file system or is not shown. The
badge counts files created since the folder was last opened; where the file
system cannot report a creation time there is no honest number, so there is
no badge. Thumbnails and icons are the ones Filer uses, from the same cache.

A folder dragged from Filer onto the dock is pinned there. Luma's file-drag
bridge carries it: a drag is all regular files or all folders and never a
mixture, because a drop would otherwise have to guess. The dock takes a
folder anywhere along it, not only on the rail, and while a folder is on its
way the rail appears with a slot to drop it into even when nothing is pinned
yet. What the source says is a folder is never trusted: every URI is
confirmed to be a directory off the main thread before anything is pinned,
as the regular-file path already confirms its own. A file dropped there is
refused with a reason rather than swallowed.

New settings, all in org.project_luma.shell-state: dock-folders,
dock-folders-visible, dock-folders-separate, dock-folders-views,
dock-folders-seen and dock-folders-detached. Hidden, the folders keep their
places, their arrangements and their counts; only the rail goes.

The rail arriving or leaving changes the dock island's length, so the
geometry pass runs again once the new length is measured. Without it the
tiles keep their old places in the input region: they are drawn where they
belong and cannot be clicked.

Generated against the Shell .120 tree with 0167-0172 applied, for release
.130.

Regenerated on top of patch 0172's .121 changes (the two places that
answer whether an island is present or shown moved).

